this here is a test paper from a fierce  competition it is from the harvard mit  math tournament now just known as hmnt  it is an annual competition for high  school students with current harvard and  mit students writing the questions and  running the event it is intended to  encourage an interest in math but they  don't go easy on anyone it is very  prestigious and attracts about a  thousand students from around the world  i tried some of the questions and let me  just say they are very tough it is an  extension beyond what is taught in  schools so sometimes to solve these you  need far flung theorems tricks and  techniques many of which i've never  encountered before and no you don't get  a calculator each year there is a  november tournament held at harvard and  then a february or march tournament held  at mit the november one is supposed to  be easier but it's not easy at all it's  just less focused on mathematical proofs  than the february one  let me show you what some of the  questions look like  this is the november general round paper  and there are 10 questions it looks like  they get harder towards the end although  the actual points given for each  question is determined by a scoring  algorithm which takes into account how  many people got that question correct it  starts off not so bad the first question  is about four different colored dominoes  red red red blue blue red and blue blue  we're asked to find the number of ways  to arrange the dominoes in a row end to  end such that adjacent ends have the  same colour the answers provided on the  website say that the answer for this one  is four from a little bit of trial and  error and extrapolating all the  possibilities  question two is testing your ability to  manipulate an algebraic expression  the answers show that if you realize  it's useful to work with the quotient  rules you can work out the possibilities  for the answer but then in my eyes they  get a lot more difficult let's go down  to number seven and i'll read it out to  you in full  we're told that there's this function  which is defined by this expression here  if x is not in the range between minus  cube root two and zero and 0 otherwise  so when x is in that range it's 0. the  sum of all real numbers x for which f 10  of x is equal to 1 can be written like  this a plus b times root c over d  where a b c d are integers  d is positive c is square free and the  greatest common denominator of a b and d  is one  find one thousand a plus 100 b plus 10c  plus d  in brackets they say here that if n of x  is the function f of x iterated n times  i couldn't keep up with their solution  that found this different expression for  f of x implying something else and then  they reverse engineer the quadratic  formula this is the rest of the solution  here and keep in mind for this paper of  10 questions you have 50 minutes and the  idea working through this in the time  limit i just thought i just can't  i just can't you can't what i can't i  can't even you can't even what i just  can't even moving on to question 8 we  have an interesting sounding question  about an ant walking around on a circle  then question 9 about a cyclic convex  pentagon  and question 10 which is suspiciously  similar to number seven it says that  real numbers x y and z satisfy these  three expressions  the largest possible value of x y z is a  plus b root c over d where a b c d are  integers find a thousand a plus a  hundred b plus ten c plus d that sounds  very similar to the question that we had  in number seven  except this time instead of being given  a function we have these three algebraic  expressions now i was inspired to take a  look at this paper by my friend pk who  runs the channel dr pk math and he's  been solving some of these problems  lately i spoke to him about this exam in  particular and he's kindly uploaded his  fully worked solution of number 10  and after seeing how he did it i can  actually understand it pk has also  uploaded his solution for question  number seven so i'll link his videos  down in the description and you can  check them out if you want to get a more  complete view of how the solutions are  done the first thing pk does is to  define a variable k to be the largest  possible value of x y z  he then takes a look at this first  expression and factors out the x  so you have x times one plus y plus y z  is equal to one then take a look at the  second expression we could replace x y z  with  k  and then take k over to that side so  rewriting this we'd have  y plus y z is equal to 2 minus k this  left hand side is pretty similar to what  we have inside this bracket here just  with an additional one so if we wanted  to know one plus y plus y z  well that would be equal to two minus k  plus one which would be three minus k  then this would tell us that  x times  three minus k  is equal to one and we can rearrange to  get that x is equal to one over three  minus k  this is one of the three important  equations that we'll need to solve the  problem we can do very similar things to  find y and z just by changing which  expression we start that process with  going through it you'd get these as the  expressions for x y and z then if you  multiplied them all together you'd get x  y z also known as k to be this  expression  this can be multiplied out and  rearranged to give you a core tip for k  and if you manage to find the roots of  this quartic equation through a  combination of guesswork factorizing  division and the quadratic formula you'd  find that they are 1  4  and 5 plus or minus square root 17 over  2. this last root is in the form that  they told us about in the question this  a plus b root c over d and k is the  largest when we take the positive of  this so we can find our a to be 5 our b  to be 1 c 17 and d is 2. adding that all  up in the way they want would give you  the answer which is written down here as  5 2 7 2. they do it a different way in  the solutions which doesn't involve  solving a cortex but in my eyes their  first step takes a much greater leap of  imagination to think of anyway this was  just the general round and there are  plenty more rounds waiting for us  is the theme round and this will be  another 10 questions in 50 minutes i'm  not sure what the theme was here maybe  it was to be as mind-bending as possible  because all the questions start with  this phrase let n be the answer to this  problem good luck with that if you can't  find the answers to the problems  question 5 says let n be the answer to  this problem the polynomial x to the n  plus a x squared plus b x plus c has  real coefficients and exactly k real  roots  find the sum of the possible values of k  and then there's a team round on here  we've got questions such as number five  how many ways are there to place 31  nights in the cells of an 8 by 8 unit  grid so that no two can attack one  another note that a knight attacks  another knight if the distance between  the centers of their cells is exactly  root five then there's something called  the gut surround and this seems probably  the most fun of the rounds you're  working in teams and it's one of those  competitions where the team sends a  runner up to go and get the questions  and they come in these groups of three  and you don't get the next set of  questions until you finish these ones  these are a varying difficulty and the  first ones are pretty easy but i like  number three it says suppose  all the letters that spell harvard  multiplied together is equal to the  letters of mit multiplied together equal  to h times m times m times t and all of  those are equal to a hundred  then find the letters multiplied  together that make up rad trivia it's a  question of trying to make some  combination of these letters up here to  be left with these ones here  and the way it can be done is to do  harvard times mit times mit divided by  hmmt  that's a hundred  a hundred  a hundred divided by a hundred  so it's a hundred squared which is ten  thousand and if you cross out all the  letters that appear on the top and the  bottom you get rad trivia now let's take  a look at the spring tournament i  believe it's usually held in february  but last year was held in march online  instead of a general round the  individual questions here are split into  three topics we have the geometry round  with questions such as  two circles with radii 71 and 100 are  externally tangent compute the largest  possible area of a right triangle whose  vertices are each on at least one of the  circles  next is the combinatorics round with  questions such as a light pole starts at  a corner of a reflective square it  bounces around inside the square  reflecting off the square's perimeter n  times before ending in a different  corner the path of the light pulse when  traced divides the square into exactly  2021 regions  compute the smallest possible value of n  and the last individual round for spring  is the algebra and number theory round  where you'll find compute the sum of all  positive integers n for which the  expression n plus 7 over the square root  of n minus 1 is an integer it starts off  somewhat comprehensible but it devolves  into very complicated questions that i'm  not even confident i'll be able to read  out correctly and then here's the team  round for the spring tournament the big  difference here to the november  tournament is that these are proof type  questions for example show this or show  that determine with proof the value of  this expression and to see what proof  answers are kind of expected to look  like let's take a look at the solutions  provided online for each question there  tends to be multiple possible solutions  i was starting to think that whatever  high school students are able to ace  these must be extremely clever wikipedia  has a list of the winners from each year  and taking a look at it i noticed that  it wasn't uncommon for the individual  champion to win multiple years in a row  for example luke robital has won in 2021  2020 and 2018 so i looked them up and i  found he actually has a youtube channel  where he solves math problems shall i  say much more advanced than the ones i  solve on my channel i also looked up a  lot of the other names and a big trend  they saw was that a lot of them have  gone on to win sometimes multiple gold  medals at the international math  olympiad so this competition attracts  people who are already performing at the  very top level i found that most of the  more recent names were currently grad  students and mathematics either at mit  or harvard or other prestigious unis but  then scrolling all the way back to the  first few years the sky ricky won over  20 years ago  so i wondered what he was up to now and  if this is the same person then he's  currently an assistant professor working  in research mathematics and that wasn't  uncommon either a few names among the  list seem to be working in maths and i  guess after all the point of this  competition was to encourage interest in  math and to show what higher math looks  like to high school students it doesn't  surprise me that the winners who had  really immersed themselves in this want  to do more than just be interested in  math but really want to turn it into  their life and their career  thanks for watching this video and i  encourage you to go and check out dr p k  math he's got heaps of exam type videos  on his channel including the two  solutions to questions from this  tournament thanks to my patrons for  making these videos possible and a  special shout out to today's patreon cat  of the day tom

